WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:06.350
E.T.: The Extra-Terrestrial is a masterpiece,
and one of the highest-grossing films of all

00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:07.350
time.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:12.589
It's a simple story, an alien gets lost in
a generic suburb, a lonely little boy named

00:00:12.589 --> 00:00:18.070
Elliot takes him in, and they establish a
profound psychic and emotional bond.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:23.260
Elliot and his siblings help E.T. "phone home"
to his planet of origin for a rescue:

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:24.480
"Home.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.800
Phone."

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.420
"E.T. phone home.”

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.640
...but first they have to make sure E.T. gets
off of Earth without being obliterated by

00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:38.670
creepy government agents.

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:43.609
Young E.T. fans who grew up into adults may
notice that the movie is a little… different

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:45.030
than they might remember.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:49.829
There are all kinds of details, great and
small, that flew right over kids' heads like

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:53.089
they were a mob of alien-protecting tweens
on bikes.

00:00:53.089 --> 00:00:57.280
These are the things you only notice in E.T.
as an adult.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.969
No grown-ups allowed!

00:00:58.969 --> 00:01:01.710
E.T.: The Extra-Terrestrial is a kids' movie.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:06.730
This isn't to say that it's a movie for kids,
but more that it is literally a movie full

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:07.730
of kids.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:12.680
It's a look into Kid World: how kids react
to divorce, how kids have their own secret

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:18.410
suburban societies, how kids conceal and help
a friendly alien visitor, and how kids fight

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:20.320
a bunch of government spooks.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:24.030
A kid might not be totally aware that it's
a big deal they're seeing themselves up on

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:28.860
that screen, because it reflects their everyday
life, which is kids-only with the occasional

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.360
adult interruption.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.690
In fact, for most of the movie, the only adult
face you'll see is that of Elliot's mother.

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:38.740
“Oh My God!"

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:43.570
Despite a lengthy classroom scene, audiences
don't see Elliot's teacher's face, and then

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.630
when adults do show up, they're mostly bad
guys.

00:01:46.630 --> 00:01:51.140
To enhance the movie's childlike point of
view, Steven Spielberg, the boy wonder of

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.090
the movies, shot a lot of the film at a kid's
eye level.

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.250
What’s your name?

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:01.420
Audiences learn a lot about Elliot over the
course of E.T.: The Extra-Terrestrial.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:06.710
He's about 11 years old, loves red-hooded
sweatshirts, lives in the suburbs, has two

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:10.229
siblings, and he can connect telepathically
with an alien.

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:14.670
As for that alien, he has a glowing finger
and a light-up heart, can bring plants back

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:19.280
to life, he likes beer and candy, and is just
trying to get home.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.370
And that's about all we know about Elliot
and E.T.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:26.640
One thing that becomes clear upon viewing
with jaded, nitpicky, adult eyes: neither

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.010
Elliot's last name, nor E.T.'s real name,
are ever given.

00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.819
“Can you, say E-T?

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:35.819
E.T."

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:36.819
"E.T."

00:02:36.819 --> 00:02:42.280
Elliot and all the other kids just call their
magical alien visitor "E.T.," short for "extra-terrestrial".

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:47.959
They can live in each other's minds, but a
simple name exchange never occurs.

00:02:47.959 --> 00:02:50.180
Obvious product placement

00:02:50.180 --> 00:02:55.070
One of the most endearing scenes in E.T. occurs
when Elliot fully befriends his little alien

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:58.790
roommate by enticing him with a trail of well-placed
Reese's Pieces.

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:04.319
The gentle, telekinetic alien at the heart
of E.T. just can't get enough of those candy-coated

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:06.130
sweetened peanut butter gems.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:09.740
Yep, to a kid's eye, this seems like a cute
and organic moment.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:23.080
To a savvy or cynical adult, however, E.T.'s
love of Reese's Pieces is a glaring, less-than-magical

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:26.040
example of Hershey-approved product placement.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:30.720
It's a wonder E.T. didn't tell Elliott he
needed to "phone home with AT&amp;T."

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:36.160
The candy company’s million-dollar promotional
gambit worked: in the immediate wake of the

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:39.450
film's success, sales of Reese's Pieces tripled.

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:43.780
Even decades later, the advertising blitz
has proven to be profitable.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:45.800
We believe you can fly

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:50.150
It's one of the most spectacular and satisfying
moments in movie history.

00:03:50.150 --> 00:03:54.819
As Elliot, his brother, and his brother's
friends race their bikes to get a weakened

00:03:54.819 --> 00:03:58.590
E.T. back to his spaceship, they come across
a roadblock of government agents that want

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.200
to take E.T. away and do who knows what to
him.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:05.190
“Tell me when it’s over!”

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:10.550
It looks like our heroes are sunk…until
E.T. uses his special powers to launch the

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:14.260
whole bicycle contingent up into the sky and
they fly away.

00:04:14.260 --> 00:04:19.250
The silhouette of E.T. and Elliot on a bike
is such an iconic image that director Steven

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:24.060
Spielberg used it as the logo for his film
production company, Amblin Entertainment.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:26.460
It Drew you in

00:04:26.460 --> 00:04:31.190
Revisiting beloved childhood movies has more
appeal than just the nostalgia factor.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:34.370
We want to see if the movies we loved as kids
"hold up."

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:38.100
Are they legitimately good, or did we just
like them because we were dumb kids?

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:45.100
Watching as an adult, you're aware of the
people behind the roles.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:50.430
For example, only an adult will realize upon
adult-aged viewing that adorable little Gertie

00:04:50.430 --> 00:04:56.510
is actually America's Sweetheart, Drew Barrymore,
star of Charlie's Angels and The Wedding Singer.

00:04:56.510 --> 00:04:58.560
What's a ‘cintus supremus’?

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.260
Some of the insults in E.T. levied by kids
make no sense at all, but only an adult's

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:07.620
ears realize that some of these words have
never been seriously used outside of E.T..

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.500
Notably, when Michael's friend Greg calls
Elliot a "cintus supremus."

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:13.580
“Cintus supremus.”

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:14.440
“Zero charisma.”

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:15.160
“Cintus supremus.”

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:16.440
"Zero charisma!"

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:17.320
"Cintus supremus!"

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:18.430
"Shut up, Greg!"

00:05:18.430 --> 00:05:22.750
While the movie’s subtitles spell out this
strange tween put-down, the words don’t

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:24.380
actually mean anything.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:29.530
Perhaps it's a bit of fake Latin, an enduring
insult-slang phenomenon best embodied by the

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:32.290
old chestnut "dorkus malorkus."

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:34.860
E.T. is kinda terrifying

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:39.130
Kids have fewer preconceived notions than
adults about the world around them, which

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:43.100
makes them often much more accepting and open-hearted
to those that are different.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:48.139
At least, that's one theme of E.T., the kids
love the little guy straightaway, and most

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:52.140
of the adults want to capture him, run tests
on him, or kill him.

00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:56.750
“Hoooome.”

00:05:56.750 --> 00:06:01.530
Here's the deal, though: Adults have every
reason to be suspicious and terrified of E.T.

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:06.520
He's a shriveled, waddling mass with huge,
glassy eyes, not to mention he's got a long,

00:06:06.520 --> 00:06:10.370
bony light-finger and a glowing, possibly
radioactive heart.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:14.660
If the average adult came across something
in the woods that looked like an E.T., they

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:18.379
wouldn't give it hugs and Reese's Pieces,
they'd run away screaming.

